WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.660
I like to get it wrong.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:07.290
We have won eight consecutive
world championships in a row

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.140
and hasn't been done by any
other team in any sport.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.910
It's difficult enough to win one,

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.130
and I think we have been able
to set the right objectives

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.843
every single year with the team.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.143
I believe that we simply got it wrong.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.960
Toto Wolff is one of those,
"If you know, you know" guys.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.180
In lots of places, he'd pass as a typical,

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.747
successful global businessman,

00:00:31.747 --> 00:00:34.947
a well dressed, multilingual
guy on the move,

00:00:34.947 --> 00:00:38.130
but for a growing segment of
the sports obsessed world,

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.510
he's a "Guess who I saw"

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.163
whip out your phone to prove it celebrity.

00:00:42.163 --> 00:00:45.030
The CEO, co-owner, and team principal

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:48.083
of the Mercedes-AMG
Petronas Formula 1 team,

00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:51.221
Wolff commands a billion
dollar enterprise,

00:00:51.221 --> 00:00:54.841
one that's implanted itself
into racing fans consciousness

00:00:54.841 --> 00:00:57.644
with an unprecedented run of success,

00:00:57.644 --> 00:01:01.173
a run that just at least took a pause.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.920
We knew that at a certain stage

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.843
that winning streak is going to end,

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.800
but all of us see this as a fantastic test

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.436
because with all the business issues

00:01:14.436 --> 00:01:18.180
and problems and challenges
that need to be faced,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.821
keeping the cool under pressure,

00:01:19.821 --> 00:01:22.710
trying to believe in
the science and trust,

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.424
it's physics, it's not mystics.

00:01:24.424 --> 00:01:27.261
But giving it the necessary
time and understanding

00:01:27.261 --> 00:01:30.960
that you can't accelerate
learning to a certain degree.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.403
That's fantastic.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.024
Formula 1, it is one of
the most difficult sports

00:01:36.024 --> 00:01:37.620
to succeed in.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:40.264
Literally everything has to
come together, man and machine,

00:01:40.264 --> 00:01:44.160
within these race weekends
and the margins of success

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.500
or failure are incredibly,
incredibly small.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.787
We're talking about thousands of seconds

00:01:48.787 --> 00:01:50.904
that could make the difference.

00:01:50.904 --> 00:01:54.803
I'm not worried about anything
in my professional life.

00:01:54.803 --> 00:01:56.700
Things happen for a reason.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.230
My perspective is beyond 10 years,

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.120
it's not just a single race.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.860
The weekends or the races we lose

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.960
are the ones our competitors
will regret the most

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.580
because we learn the most.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.880
It's gonna make us better.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.320
In racing, if you're not
winning, you're losing.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.640
It's just that simple.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.170
And after one of the most trying years

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:16.560
in the team's history, Wolff
is hell bent on showing

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.033
he still knows how to win.

00:02:27.275 --> 00:02:29.160
Monaco occupies a quirky spot

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.200
in the collective global imagination,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.126
yachts, casinos, and of course fast cars.

00:02:34.126 --> 00:02:37.370
It's tiny, the second
smallest country in the world.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.080
So small that when you're
driving in from the airport

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.393
in nearby Nice, if you miss the exit,

00:02:42.393 --> 00:02:45.369
you could end up in Italy theoretically.

00:02:45.369 --> 00:02:48.153
Like the signage has changed.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:54.713
Once you're there,

00:02:54.713 --> 00:02:56.340
it has a fairytale quality,

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.350
right down to the palace
that sits on the hill

00:02:58.350 --> 00:02:59.760
overlooking the the harbor.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.881
There's an actual prince
living there with his family

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:04.457
and Prince Albert's family has long been

00:03:04.457 --> 00:03:08.041
beyond supportive of the sport
that put Monaco on the map,

00:03:08.041 --> 00:03:09.750
but I'm here to meet up with someone

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.078
many consider modern F1 royalty.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:25.763
I got you.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:28.193
Copy me.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.878
What they used to do in the
past is that they just drove

00:03:40.878 --> 00:03:42.540
with doors open,

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:43.890
but they need to be careful

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.400
that you're not crashing anybody out.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.543
Pedestrian as well.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:51.683
Wow.

00:03:52.554 --> 00:03:53.387
And leave it open.

00:03:53.387 --> 00:03:56.995
That is much more pleasant
on a hot day in Monaco.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.977
I mean obviously this
car attracts attention,

00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:06.835
but you now attract attention.

00:04:06.835 --> 00:04:10.500
Actually, it's an
interesting life experiment

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.740
for me because I'm not a sports person

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.019
that grew up with that.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:19.519
I think let's close it for the time being.

00:04:21.465 --> 00:04:25.290
That kind of public persona
happened to me much later

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:29.850
in my life where I think my
character was already done

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.310
and couldn't influence
me in a negative way.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:34.990
It shows that the popularity of the sport

00:04:36.472 --> 00:04:39.181
and of the team and public recognition

00:04:39.181 --> 00:04:42.255
will be always something
that is flattering

00:04:42.255 --> 00:04:44.343
and you mustn't take it for granted.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.210
Tell me about Monaco.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:53.130
I mean this is the cradle
in many ways of this sport.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:54.150
How do you view it?

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:55.200
Formula 1 is Monaco.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.532
Monaco is Formula 1.

00:04:56.532 --> 00:05:01.480
If you ask someone that
has heard about Formula 1

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:03.750
and knows a little bit about it,

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.850
they will associate Formula 1 with Monaco.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.760
It's by far the most popular race.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.451
Now, obviously Miami was
great and Las Vegas coming in,

00:05:13.451 --> 00:05:15.480
hopefully they're gonna go,

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:16.672
they're gonna be big as well,

00:05:16.672 --> 00:05:20.640
but Monaco was always a glamorous town

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.833
that was known for Formula 1.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.740
Wolff, who grew up in Austria,
picked up racing at 17,

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.140
but by 1994, his performance on the track

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.690
sent him searching for another challenge.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:35.016
He looked to business.

00:05:35.016 --> 00:05:37.770
As a young man, if you
can't be a racing driver,

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:38.970
you'd like to be an investment banker.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:42.921
That at least was I how it happened to me.

00:05:42.921 --> 00:05:47.726
In the late 1990s, the
internet came out and with it,

00:05:47.726 --> 00:05:50.962
the boom of startup businesses

00:05:50.962 --> 00:05:53.280
with valuations going astronomical,

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.170
a little bit to what we have experienced

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:56.400
until most recently.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.136
And I jumped on that bandwagon.

00:05:59.136 --> 00:06:02.670
Through investing firms
MarchFifteen and MarchSixteen,

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.950
he enjoyed the success of the dot com era

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.770
and also the less sexy world of investing

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.450
in industrial companies.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:11.640
His success in the
investment world allowed him

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.223
to get back into the business of racing.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.340
And then came the call.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.060
Do you want to become a partner

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.330
in a Mercedes Formula 1 team?

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:24.009
And literally, I had to pinch myself.

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:25.740
I'm very excited, obviously,

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.579
to be representing
Mercedes-Benz in Formula 1.

00:06:28.579 --> 00:06:30.840
In 2013, Toto would take over management

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.383
of the Mercedes F1 team,

00:06:32.383 --> 00:06:34.599
but his investments in the team also meant

00:06:34.599 --> 00:06:37.428
that he would own about a
third of the overall franchise,

00:06:37.428 --> 00:06:40.521
a good investment considering
under his leadership,

00:06:40.521 --> 00:06:43.908
the Mercedes team would go
on an unprecedented run.

00:06:43.908 --> 00:06:46.530
Lewis Hamilton would win seven straight

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.140
Drivers' Championships and
Mercedes would win eight

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:50.580
Constructors' titles.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.950
That's the team prize that
takes into account point totals

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.617
from each team's two drivers.

00:06:54.617 --> 00:06:57.281
It was a level of
domination rarely seen in F1

00:06:57.281 --> 00:06:59.892
or any sport for that matter.

00:06:59.892 --> 00:07:01.800
Toto would also turned the sport

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.600
into a true family business.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:04.710
Marrying his wife Susie,

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:06.801
herself a former British pro driver.

00:07:06.801 --> 00:07:10.410
She most recently ran a team
on the Formula E Circuit.

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:12.233
That's the electric
car division of the FIA

00:07:12.233 --> 00:07:15.338
the governing body that
also includes Formula One.

00:07:15.338 --> 00:07:18.450
I got to catch up with the
couple just over the border

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:21.330
in France with a view
you might only get dining

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:23.633
with the ultimate motorsport power couple.

00:07:23.633 --> 00:07:26.850
So what is it like for
the two of you to work

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:28.290
in the same business?

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:29.727
Well, I can't speak for Toto,

00:07:29.727 --> 00:07:31.983
but I certainly, I love what he does.

00:07:31.983 --> 00:07:34.080
I'm, I'm very, very proud.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:37.063
But I also love following
it from the sidelines

00:07:37.063 --> 00:07:38.490
of watching the races,

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:39.570
coming to some races.

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:42.150
But I also love, I mean, from him,

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:43.800
I learned so much over the years,

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:45.180
which was part of the reason why I felt

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.823
that I could take on
the role in Formula E.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:51.330
Susie started racing
even earlier than Toto,

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:52.736
when she was about eight years old,

00:07:52.736 --> 00:07:55.421
decided to become a racing
driver at the age of 13.

00:07:55.421 --> 00:07:57.990
Susie and Toto's paths
crossed when she was

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.156
with Mercedes-Benz and
German Touring Cars.

00:08:00.156 --> 00:08:02.670
I went to a race in New York

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:06.270
and I remember standing
being able to see the Statue

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.280
of Liberty with these electric racing cars

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:09.600
and obviously then Diesel-gate happened

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.900
and everything sped up in
the automotive industry.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:14.880
And then I thought,
this has got something.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:17.610
And I literally just copy
and pasted Toto's deal

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:19.980
with Mercedes said, "Okay,
I don't need a salary,

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.087
but I want equity."

00:08:21.087 --> 00:08:23.550
I remember there was one
instance at the beginning

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:25.230
in Formula E, we were struggling

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:28.110
and I had both cars in the
top and one after the other,

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:31.183
they all stopped with the same
problem with the drive shaft.

00:08:31.183 --> 00:08:32.910
And I was getting phone calls from people,

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:36.000
obviously very unhappy
that we had this issue.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.310
And then I called Toto and
he said, "What happened?"

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:39.780
I said, "We have this issue
with the drive shaft."

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:41.011
And he said, "Sort it."

00:08:41.011 --> 00:08:43.530
I said, "Well, yeah, I better try sort it,

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:44.610
but I'm not the technical director.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:45.480
I don't even know what the issue is."

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.320
And he's like, "It's your
problem, you have to sort it."

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.330
But it was also in that
moment that I realized

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:52.560
that the buck stopped with you.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:57.560
It's about very quickly fixing
issues and finding solutions.

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:58.860
During his tenure with Mercedes,

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:01.200
Wolff has become a master
at solving problems

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:03.353
and managing talent, including Hamilton,

00:09:03.353 --> 00:09:06.270
indisputably the most
famous driver on the circuit

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:07.294
over the past decade.

00:09:07.294 --> 00:09:10.260
Wolff's role as both owner and manager

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:12.679
of a record setting sports
franchise is unique.

00:09:12.679 --> 00:09:15.840
Imagine if the Patriots'
Robert Kraft and Bill Belichick

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:18.570
were the same person
which is how Toto ended up

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:21.513
teaching a case about himself
at Harvard Business School.

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.500
Someone like a Toto Wolff,
any team principal really,

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.720
is responsible for
hundreds of people, right?

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:31.530
Mercedes has 1800 people
across the factory

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:33.396
and the unit where they
actually look after the engines.

00:09:33.396 --> 00:09:36.797
So 1800 people who he has to manage

00:09:36.797 --> 00:09:40.560
and what struck me is
the remarkable attention

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:42.327
to detail that he brings to the table.

00:09:42.327 --> 00:09:44.324
The governing body, the FIA,

00:09:44.324 --> 00:09:48.870
is set up to not have anyone
win eight times in a row.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:51.600
This is all about trying to
maintain a competitive balance.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.520
So against all those odds

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.031
in an incredibly difficult environment,

00:09:55.031 --> 00:09:59.010
the Mercedes team was able to
keep a winning streak alive

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:00.390
for eight years.

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:03.090
That I thought was a
really interesting premise

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:04.080
for a case study.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:06.588
Anita Elberse is a Harvard
Business School professor

00:10:06.588 --> 00:10:08.876
specializing in the
intersection of entertainment,

00:10:08.876 --> 00:10:10.297
media, and sports.

00:10:10.297 --> 00:10:13.530
She's written case studies
on figures like LeBron James,

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:15.553
Maria Sharapova, the Rock, and Beyonce.

00:10:15.553 --> 00:10:17.569
Putting people at the center,

00:10:17.569 --> 00:10:21.090
analyzing mistakes very rigorously,

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:24.180
having an open atmosphere in
which everyone can speak up

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:26.040
if they see something
that needs to be fixed,

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:28.590
and then also actually
having the willingness

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:30.998
to try to fix what has been going wrong,

00:10:30.998 --> 00:10:33.870
this relentless pursuit of winning

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:35.943
and never giving into complacency.

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:41.190
The last 10 years have been
a critical growth period

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:42.720
for Formula 1 racing.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.480
Spurred by the $4.4 billion purchase of F1

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:48.106
by John Malone's Liberty Media in 2017.

00:10:48.106 --> 00:10:51.180
Toto saw firsthand how much the ambition

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:52.873
for the sport accelerated.

00:10:52.873 --> 00:10:54.697
With Liberty coming in,

00:10:54.697 --> 00:10:56.790
although we were skeptical
at the beginning,

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:59.790
that's where we really started
to have this massive upswing

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:01.980
and together with the
governing body, the FIA,

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.940
we were able to put on
a show during Covid,

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:07.604
that's the only global sport
and it obviously helped a lot.

00:11:07.604 --> 00:11:09.690
The business of Formula 1,

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:11.700
it's a bit more complicated
than it might seem

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:12.860
from just watching a race.

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:15.570
There's the global governing
body for the 10 teams,

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:17.972
each of which is an
independently run business.

00:11:17.972 --> 00:11:19.969
Like pro basketball or football teams,

00:11:19.969 --> 00:11:22.326
each has its own ownership group.

00:11:22.326 --> 00:11:25.150
Those teams get cuts of
overall Formula 1 revenue

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:27.942
based on each season's performance.

00:11:27.942 --> 00:11:30.960
Those teams also have
sponsors who pay for the right

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:32.610
to have their names on the cars, helmets,

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:35.070
or other team materials like crew shirts.

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:36.366
Finally, there are promoters.

00:11:36.366 --> 00:11:39.210
They buy the right from F1 to host races

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:40.620
in individual locations,

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:42.073
usually for several years at a time.

00:11:42.073 --> 00:11:44.559
They recoup their money and earn profits

00:11:44.559 --> 00:11:47.449
from selling hospitality
and other sponsorships.

00:11:47.449 --> 00:11:49.470
Since buying F1 in 2017,

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:52.551
Liberty has grown revenue
for the circuit by 20%,

00:11:52.551 --> 00:11:54.419
despite a global pandemic.

00:11:54.419 --> 00:11:58.417
Revenue totaled $2.1 billion in 2021.

00:11:58.417 --> 00:12:01.100
Being a shareholder in a
company and managing it,

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:03.711
it is always about profitable growth.

00:12:03.711 --> 00:12:07.167
Nevertheless, the biggest
part of the return

00:12:07.167 --> 00:12:10.530
on investment was the advertising value

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:12.798
that we generated from
Mercedes and our partners.

00:12:12.798 --> 00:12:17.607
Lap time and performance always
outweighed profitability.

00:12:17.607 --> 00:12:20.070
Perhaps the biggest change came

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:22.196
with a radically
disrupted media landscape.

00:12:22.196 --> 00:12:24.060
So it's helpful to have one

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:25.650
of the world's most influential media

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:27.759
and communications
companies as your owner.

00:12:27.759 --> 00:12:31.653
What are you doing?
Just sending good vibes.

00:12:31.653 --> 00:12:33.390
And he is through.
See you later.

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:35.040
Social media, staunchly resisted

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:36.158
by the previous management,

00:12:36.158 --> 00:12:39.570
became a new vehicle for
promotion and to connect teams

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:41.040
and drivers to their fans,

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:42.780
augmenting, even supplanting,

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.600
the once stodgy single
broadcast for each race.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.306
Now instead of engaging a
couple dozen Sundays a year,

00:12:48.306 --> 00:12:51.474
fans had deep and direct ongoing access.

00:12:51.474 --> 00:12:54.483
And then there was that show.

00:12:55.950 --> 00:12:57.939
All I do is pray for a safe race.

00:12:57.939 --> 00:13:01.500
You need to respect the
speed for the second.

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:04.852
A driver has fear, you need to retire.

00:13:04.852 --> 00:13:07.281
The Mercedes team wasn't included

00:13:07.281 --> 00:13:09.690
in the first season of "Drive to Survive."

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:10.953
That was Toto's decision.

00:13:10.953 --> 00:13:13.037
One of the reasons I didn't want to do it

00:13:13.037 --> 00:13:16.101
'cause I didn't want our
engineers and drivers

00:13:16.101 --> 00:13:18.630
to act like in a movie
Yeah, right.

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:21.390
rather than being fully
concentrated on the job.

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:22.950
While capturing failures on camera

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:25.337
might not have been ideal
for Toto and his team,

00:13:25.337 --> 00:13:28.484
for the sport of F1, it's
been a massive success.

00:13:28.484 --> 00:13:31.200
After seeing what one
season did for the sport

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:32.196
and his rivals,

00:13:32.196 --> 00:13:35.523
he signed up for season two
and an entirely new level

00:13:35.523 --> 00:13:39.684
of fame and notoriety for his
team and for him followed.

00:13:39.684 --> 00:13:41.490
It launched a boom

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:43.356
and it opened up a
completely new audience,

00:13:43.356 --> 00:13:46.250
different to the hardcore fans,

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:48.377
especially in the United States.

00:13:48.377 --> 00:13:50.844
With Austin and Miami
already on the calendar,

00:13:50.844 --> 00:13:55.106
in 2023, F1 will return after
four decades to Las Vegas

00:13:55.106 --> 00:13:57.270
for a nighttime race

00:13:57.270 --> 00:13:59.554
that will include the famous Vegas strip.

00:13:59.554 --> 00:14:02.018
It's quickly becoming one of
the hottest tickets in sports.

00:14:02.018 --> 00:14:05.566
With the three US races
and five in North America,

00:14:05.566 --> 00:14:07.433
including Montreal and Mexico City,

00:14:07.433 --> 00:14:10.318
the sport is building
a massive US audience,

00:14:10.318 --> 00:14:13.001
which is always part of
Liberty Media's business case.

00:14:13.001 --> 00:14:17.400
The races on US soil are
proving wildly popular

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:18.780
in terms of attendance,

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:21.257
drawing some of the biggest
crowds across the circuit.

00:14:21.257 --> 00:14:25.680
Miami in its first year
attracted 330,000 fans

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:26.786
across the weekend.

00:14:26.786 --> 00:14:29.370
Austin was the single
biggest event of the year

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:32.313
with 440,000 spectators.

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:38.887
We arrived in Miami and there was

00:14:40.018 --> 00:14:44.100
lots of young screaming
women, "Toto, Toto, Toto"

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:46.246
and I thought, wow, that's new.

00:14:46.246 --> 00:14:49.001
Because before,

00:14:49.001 --> 00:14:50.373
young women would kind
of come up to me and say,

00:14:50.373 --> 00:14:52.200
"Oh, I love your racing."

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.240
But now they come up
like, "Oh, I love Toto.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:55.073
I love your husband."

00:14:55.073 --> 00:14:56.607
I'm like, "Yeah, so do I."

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:00.990
This is a season like no other.

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:02.490
Unbelievable start from Hamilton.

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:04.110
It's just insane.

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:05.060
Oh, they touched.

00:15:07.843 --> 00:15:10.470
The growing audience was
used to the weak tweak drama

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:12.060
of the paddock and the pits,

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:16.440
but nothing could prepare fans
or teams for the 2021 season.

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:19.020
It culminated in beyond dramatic fashion

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:21.930
with the Driver Championship
decided in the final race

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:24.963
in Abu Dhabi on a judgment
call by the official.

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:33.960
What happened in Abu
Dhabi at the last race

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:35.889
really captivated people
even if you weren't

00:15:35.889 --> 00:15:37.410
into motor sport.

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:39.480
I mean, the battle, how ferocious it got.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.420
There are five lapped cars standing

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:44.190
between him and the race leader.

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:45.660
It's these magnificent brands.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:49.470
It's these gladiators on
track giving it everything.

00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:51.240
All the cars ahead of you are allowed

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:53.278
to overtake Hamilton.

00:15:53.278 --> 00:15:55.077
It's just everything is
happening in that paddock.

00:15:55.077 --> 00:15:55.910
No, no.

00:15:57.492 --> 00:15:59.700
All the politics,
This is a mess.

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:01.500
the interaction among the stakeholders.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:03.060
Mike, that is so not right.

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:04.410
That is so not right.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.960
You think you have the
championship, then it's lost.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:08.550
This is it.
At a certain stage in Jeddah,

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:10.166
the three races before the end,

00:16:10.166 --> 00:16:13.380
I smashed my headphones because
I thought in that minute,

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:15.966
Lewis was break tested
and we lost championship.

00:16:15.966 --> 00:16:18.880
And then you realize 10 seconds
later, it's actually on.

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:20.843
He just needs to win the race.

00:16:20.843 --> 00:16:24.510
We are letting Netflix in
and they're seeing things

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:27.467
that otherwise if you look at
the pure sports broadcasting,

00:16:27.467 --> 00:16:28.680
you wouldn't realize.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:30.540
That by itself, Formula 1 is drama

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:32.526
and have always been
on track and off track.

00:16:32.526 --> 00:16:34.980
There's too much at stake
and all that's being captured

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:35.853
by the lenses.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:39.541
Toto says he hasn't watched
more than a couple episodes

00:16:39.541 --> 00:16:41.992
of the show and has not
watched the final episode

00:16:41.992 --> 00:16:43.518
of last season.

00:16:43.518 --> 00:16:45.780
I certainly didn't want to be reminded,

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:47.490
but I felt we needed to watch it once.

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:49.119
And I watched of course the last episode

00:16:49.119 --> 00:16:50.667
and Toto said, "Do you
think I should watch?"

00:16:50.667 --> 00:16:53.046
I was like, "You know what, don't.

00:16:53.046 --> 00:16:54.960
I've done it for both of us."

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:57.353
Yeah.
It's done and dusted.

00:16:57.353 --> 00:16:59.280
The new season not only
brought little relief,

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:00.309
but more challenges.

00:17:00.309 --> 00:17:02.723
New regulations required a new car design

00:17:02.723 --> 00:17:04.646
and Mercedes simply got it wrong

00:17:04.646 --> 00:17:07.979
in a way that no in-season
tinkering could fix.

00:17:07.979 --> 00:17:11.820
A cost cap was introduced
in the 2021 season

00:17:11.820 --> 00:17:13.710
and the jury is still
out on how much parity

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:15.270
it will bring to the circuit.

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:17.580
It did mean Mercedes
couldn't simply throw money

00:17:17.580 --> 00:17:18.923
at its design mistake.

00:17:18.923 --> 00:17:21.150
In the end, Mercedes failed

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:22.800
to win a ninth straight team title

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:25.230
and neither of its drivers
was ever in real contention

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:26.502
for the Drivers' Championship.

00:17:26.502 --> 00:17:28.533
To make it worse for Toto and his team,

00:17:28.533 --> 00:17:31.770
arch rival Red Bull added
a constructor's title

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:34.773
to Max Verstappen's second
straight Drivers' Championship.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:37.624
The winning streak has
definitely come to an end

00:17:37.624 --> 00:17:40.650
and I'm sure it's been a
really tough year for everyone

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:41.610
at Mercedes.

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:43.812
What goes to the very heart
of why they are so successful

00:17:43.812 --> 00:17:46.390
is this idea of realizing that losing

00:17:46.390 --> 00:17:48.270
is always just around the corner,

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:50.296
that the winning at some
point will have to end.

00:17:50.296 --> 00:17:52.440
They were so rigorous in their approach.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:54.563
They always analyze
everything, win or lose.

00:17:54.563 --> 00:17:56.363
They were never complacent

00:17:56.363 --> 00:17:58.955
and I think that sets them up really well

00:17:58.955 --> 00:18:00.933
for future success.

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:17.713
So on race day, this office

00:18:17.713 --> 00:18:20.646
is live onto the race track.

00:18:20.646 --> 00:18:23.220
Toto's main office is at team headquarters

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:26.212
in North Hamptonshire, couple
hours outside of London.

00:18:26.212 --> 00:18:28.800
This is where the majority
of the team employees work

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:31.830
and only a very small portion
ever see cars on the track

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:33.983
in person on a regular basis.

00:18:33.983 --> 00:18:37.890
When I'm sitting on my
station at the racetrack,

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:39.330
all the engineers,

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:41.850
we talk to the various
channels and it can well be

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:44.118
that the engineers are
sitting very much here,

00:18:44.118 --> 00:18:46.170
I wouldn't know the difference.

00:18:46.170 --> 00:18:47.657
Right.
And whatever time zone

00:18:47.657 --> 00:18:50.595
we are, you can see that it's 30 engineers

00:18:50.595 --> 00:18:53.620
that are sitting here
working live on strategy,

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:56.122
giving us live information
about the state of the car

00:18:56.122 --> 00:19:00.317
and all what we see on the
track is right in front of us

00:19:00.317 --> 00:19:01.863
for them to see.

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:06.043
So my channels are basically open to,

00:19:06.043 --> 00:19:08.220
I would say probably 50 engineers

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:12.150
that I could have a directed
discussion with if I wanted.

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:13.110
But also for us,

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:16.896
we have a a strict intercom
protocol that we follow

00:19:16.896 --> 00:19:19.803
and you can say it's
almost like in an airplane.

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:25.123
Our ambition is to win every single one,

00:19:25.123 --> 00:19:29.700
but in our sport it's about
physics and not mystics.

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:31.349
We got it wrong with the physics this year

00:19:31.349 --> 00:19:34.920
and it is about how can we recover?

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:37.435
I see that as a real opportunity for us

00:19:37.435 --> 00:19:40.346
because all of these people have been here

00:19:40.346 --> 00:19:42.331
when they saw how good it was going

00:19:42.331 --> 00:19:45.300
and now how difficult it can be

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:47.070
and that's gonna be an important learning

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:49.080
from them also in the future.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:52.530
You will not take it for granted
that winning a championship

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:53.610
is easy.

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:55.470
So I believe there's always something good

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:56.765
in getting it wrong,

00:19:56.765 --> 00:20:00.903
even though it feels
difficult when it happens.

00:20:07.988 --> 00:20:10.074
But if I can't keep up.

00:20:10.074 --> 00:20:10.907
No.

00:20:16.838 --> 00:20:18.120
Have you ever been on a track?

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:19.967
I have never been on a track.

00:20:19.967 --> 00:20:21.150
Never?
Never.

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:22.530
So what do I need to know?

00:20:22.530 --> 00:20:24.113
Just hold on tight?
Hold on tight.

00:20:25.191 --> 00:20:27.720
I like that we're racing Lewis though.

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:29.197
That's not so bad, no?

00:20:29.197 --> 00:20:31.688
No, not a bad way to spend a Tuesday.

00:20:38.643 --> 00:20:39.570
The Circuit of the Americas,

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:41.220
as the Austin racetrack is known,

00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:43.248
is empty on this scorching summer day,

00:20:43.248 --> 00:20:45.720
save for a few dozen Mercedes personnel

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:48.618
and some very excited Mercedes
sponsors from CrowdStrike.

00:20:48.618 --> 00:20:50.379
Toto, along with Hamilton,

00:20:50.379 --> 00:20:52.733
are hosting top CrowdStrike executives

00:20:52.733 --> 00:20:55.563
for what feels like the
ultimate sponsor perk,

00:20:55.563 --> 00:20:57.660
driving around an F1 track

00:20:57.660 --> 00:20:59.220
with the world's most famous driver

00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:00.838
and his also very famous boss.

00:21:00.838 --> 00:21:03.000
It's more evidence of the big money

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:06.829
and big audience opportunity
for Mercedes and F1 in America.

00:21:06.829 --> 00:21:10.803
US continues to fall in love
with the spectacle and drama.

00:21:12.338 --> 00:21:13.920
In the past it would've
been a sticker on the car

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:17.670
and I give you visibility and
Formula 1 was very successful

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:20.317
at that because we are the
only true big global sport

00:21:20.317 --> 00:21:22.530
and what has happened
over the last few years

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:26.202
that our audiences in the US
literally went through the sky.

00:21:26.202 --> 00:21:30.882
We generate about a billion
and a half audience every year,

00:21:30.882 --> 00:21:34.740
but it's involved to much more
of an integrated partnership

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:36.750
and it's not a sponsorship
anymore in that sense.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:39.150
We have companies that
are working together.

00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:41.130
We try to increase sales.

00:21:41.130 --> 00:21:44.816
We utilize our network to
really help the business itself,

00:21:44.816 --> 00:21:46.722
but our largest countries

00:21:46.722 --> 00:21:50.100
are about 100 million live
audience, some of them.

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:51.210
And Austin is a good example,

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:53.310
more than 400,000 people
across the weekend

00:21:53.310 --> 00:21:54.990
and the marketing side,

00:21:54.990 --> 00:21:56.880
the visibility basically
is the icing on the cake

00:21:56.880 --> 00:21:59.160
and just comes on top.
Wow.

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:01.650
I think we have been able to really shape

00:22:01.650 --> 00:22:04.413
and identify our core values.

00:22:05.550 --> 00:22:08.370
I feel we are in a good
place and having three races

00:22:08.370 --> 00:22:11.610
in the us, one in Mexico
and one in Brazil.

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:15.210
It gives us a good footprint
in the Americas, beyond Europe,

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:18.543
Australia, Asia, and it
justifies it as a global sport.

00:22:24.778 --> 00:22:26.790
This is a good setting.
Okay.

00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:28.110
You can really go full throttle.

00:22:28.110 --> 00:22:29.370
It'll get a little bit of rotation,

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:31.070
but it won't spin out.
Perfect.

00:22:33.503 --> 00:22:36.000
With every lap, every
overseas trip in a season

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:37.260
that's only expanding,

00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:40.212
next year there will be
24 races around the world,

00:22:40.212 --> 00:22:43.111
Toto moves ever closer to a crossroads.

00:22:43.111 --> 00:22:45.690
He signed a three year contract extension

00:22:45.690 --> 00:22:46.923
at the end of 2020.

00:22:46.923 --> 00:22:50.383
Moving out of day to day after
that and remaining an owner,

00:22:50.383 --> 00:22:52.160
likely a vocal and interested one,

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:54.237
feels like a real possibility.

00:22:54.237 --> 00:22:57.852
For now, his sole focus
as F1 gets more global

00:22:57.852 --> 00:23:01.683
and more profitable is
getting Mercedes back on top.

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:06.870
I was a racing driver
turned finance person,

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:10.327
came back as a team
owner and team manager,

00:23:10.327 --> 00:23:12.153
and I'm also the trainer.

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:15.840
I love the sport and this
is what I enjoy doing

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:16.673
at the moment,

00:23:16.673 --> 00:23:18.300
but I feel when it's
the fifth time in a row

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:20.081
and you're finishing second or third,

00:23:20.081 --> 00:23:22.530
rather than winning, these things,

00:23:22.530 --> 00:23:24.450
these negative emotions still kick in.

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:27.720
At the end, the joint
target, the joint objective,

00:23:27.720 --> 00:23:30.690
it's so massive that we just
need to remind ourselves

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:32.935
in these moments that
we just want the same.

00:23:32.935 --> 00:23:34.890
We're just seeing the solution somewhere.

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:36.240
So we just need to get together

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.140
and say what is it that we want to do?

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:42.120
Data is important to develop a quick car,

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:43.530
but data don't make decisions.

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:44.363
Humans do.

